From: Matt C <latentcollapse@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 22:58:17 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 04/42] Fix goal-reactivation silent failure and contaminated
 tool description

Second-round adversarial review found that the previous commit's
diagnosis was correct but nothing was actually fixed. This is the fix,
not another description of one.

- agent-session.ts: an active goal used to unconditionally push
  "ipython" into the active tool list during _buildRuntime, even in a
  baseToolsOverride (NEURA-mode) session with no kernel and no ipython
  tool definition. setActiveToolsByName silently drops unknown tool
  names (no else branch on the registry lookup), so the goal system
  believed it had granted ipython access and hadn't, with no error
  anywhere. Now throws the same "requires the ipython tool" error
  _ensureGoalRuntimeActive already uses for the equivalent explicit
  path, instead of silently degrading. No behavior change for any
  session that has a real kernel (this only fires when one doesn't).

- neurabash.ts: the tool description told the model to "prefer
  ipython" for sub-agent spawning, skills, and persistent state,
  unconditionally, in every mode including NEURA. Fixed on the
  shipping tool itself, not a test-only variant: removed the
  cross-tool preference claim, replaced with an honest statement of
  NeuraBash's own current limitations (ephemeral per call, no
  sub-agent/diff/attachment support yet). A tool's description
  shouldn't presume what else exists in the session it's loaded into.

- neurabash-substrate-modes.test.ts: rewritten to call
  createAgentSession directly (the real public entry point) instead
  of going through the harness's direct AgentSession construction,
  which never exercised the SDK plumbing from the prior commit. Added
  a positive observable for kernel non-construction
  (session.getToolDefinition("ipython") is undefined, not just
  inactive) and a regression test for the goal fix above. 4/4 passing.

